=====================================
FOLDER: LFER_3_mg2
SYSTEM: MareNostrum 5 — glogin2
CREATED: 16/06/2026
UPDATED: 17/09/2026
PURPOSE: Production LFER study of pyrophosphate hydrolysis with Mg2+, comparing
         the pph4 and pph3 protonation states, plus the transition-state level
         diagram spanning the whole PPi protonation spectrum.
=====================================

--- OVERVIEW: LFER_3_mg2 ---

Production Linear Free Energy Relationship (LFER) study of pyrophosphate hydrolysis in the
presence of Mg2+, comparing the pph4 (fully protonated) and pph3 (tri-protonated) systems.

o NNP/       the iteration-005 NNP committee driving every run in this folder — see its
             README (and ../NNP_LFER_choice/ for the iteration-004 consistency check that
             preceded it).
o codes/     shared free-energy-surface reconstruction scripts (FES_2D*.py,
             FES_from_Reweighting.py) — see its README.
o pph4/      the main system: OPES metadynamics (2D FES) plus three SMD->US->WHAM PMF
             pipelines mapping the full multi-step reaction free-energy profile —
             see pph4/README.
o pph3/      benchmark OPES metadynamics hydrolysis run for the tri-protonated system,
             for comparison against pph4 — see pph3/README.
o ppimg2/    reserved/empty — not yet populated at the time of this review.

Note: this README documents pre-existing content; no existing file was modified.

06/08/2026

o Read-only review of the pph4 and pph3 OPES hydrolysis PLUMED inputs to tabulate the
  reaction-CV walls (cowh, cobh, coelp, dobp, dowp) alongside the equivalent setups in
  Elia's 'training' and 'Training_2Mgcomplex' folders. Details and the extracted values
  are recorded in pph4/README and pph3/README. Backups of the three READMEs touched in
  this pass were left as 'README.bak_20260806'.

26/08/2026 - 12:29

o Went back to the transition-state level diagram produced by 'TS_barplot.py'. The
  original 'ts_levels.png' comes out at 24 x 8 inches, so the level bars take up a small
  fraction of each column and the canvas reads as stretched and mostly empty. I also
  wanted the ten species to share one coherent palette instead of the mixture of
  colour-blind-safe hues and stock matplotlib colour names that had accumulated there.

o Rather than edit 'TS_barplot.py' I added 'TS_barplot_v2.py' next to it. It reads the
  same 'ts_energies.txt' and writes 'ts_levels_v2.png'; the old script and its output are
  untouched. The colours are now chemistry-encoded: a viridis ramp indexed by protonation
  state for the purely protonated species, with each alpha isomer drawn as the lighter
  tint of its beta partner, and a separate warm ramp for the three Mg-bearing species, so
  the protonation gradient and the Mg series can be told apart at a glance.

o The layout is rebuilt around the rule that no text may ever overlap another text, a
  bar, or one of the dashed lines separating the species. Each species now sits in its own
  box delimited by light grey dashed lines. An isolated level keeps the bar centred with
  the TS name on the left and the energy on the right. A near-degenerate pair is split
  horizontally: the higher level shifted right with its name on top of the bar and its
  energy to the left, the lower level shifted left with its name under the bar and its
  energy to the right. Levels that are exactly degenerate are drawn side by side, each
  with its own name on top, sharing a single energy label on the left carrying both
  uncertainties slash-separated. Three or more levels crowded inside a single text line
  get one slot each at their true energies and are joined by leader lines to a de-crowded
  label list at the right of the box.

o For the present data that means the P2O7 levels at 42.1, 41.9 and 41.3 are the
  leader-line case; the 25.0 tie in beta-H3P2O7 is drawn as two side-by-side bars with
  'TS5' passing through the clear channel between them; and the close pairs in H4P2O7
  (22.5 / 21.9) and beta-H3P2O7 are the shifted case.

o Worth recording a bug in 'TS_barplot.py': its 'spread_labels' function nudged the label
  text vertically to resolve collisions while leaving the bar at its true energy, so in
  the P2O7 column the '41.9 +/- 0.6' text did not actually sit at 41.9. The new script
  drops that function and moves bars rather than text, so a label is always at the energy
  of its own level.

o The canvas is no longer a fixed size. Column widths are measured from the actually
  rendered text extents, and the figure is grown only until a verification pass finds no
  remaining overlap; for this data it settles at about 18.9 x 12.2 inches, an aspect ratio
  of roughly 1.55 instead of the previous 3.0. The script refuses to save the figure if
  that verification pass still reports a collision.

o Ran 'TS_barplot_v2.py' on glogin2 against the Anaconda matplotlib 3.8.0 build and it
  produced 'ts_levels_v2.png'. A backup of this README as it stood before this entry was
  left as 'README.bak_20260826'.

26/08/2026 - 15:48

o Three adjustments to 'TS_barplot_v2.py' after looking at the first render.

o In the P2O7 column the three crowded levels (42.1, 41.9, 41.3) were being spread
  into separate horizontal slots. They are now kept aligned on the column axis like
  every other bar, so the whole column shares one vertical, and only the leader lines
  fan out to the de-crowded energy labels on the right. The referencing by lines is
  unchanged.

o The rule that displaces a near-degenerate pair no longer fires on a proximity
  threshold. The script now lays the pair out in the plain centred style first,
  measures it, and keeps that layout whenever the labels genuinely clear one another;
  the horizontal shift is applied only if a real overlap is found. With the present
  data this means the H4P2O7 pair at 22.5 and 21.9 goes back to the default rendering,
  one directly above the other, which is legible as it stands. The shifted layout
  remains in the script for data where it is actually needed.

o The plot is now enclosed in a solid black frame on all four sides. The light grey
  dashed lines are used only for the internal dividers between species, where before
  they also formed the top and right edges of the plot.

o Re-ran on glogin2; 'ts_levels_v2.png' now comes out at about 18.1 x 12.1 inches.
  'TS_barplot.py' and 'ts_levels.png' remain untouched.

14/09/2026

o Came back to 'TS_barplot.py' because the figure it produces, once dropped into a
  presentation slide, is hard to read: at 16 x 8 inches with 9 pt labels the text and the
  level bars occupy only a small part of each column and nothing is legible from a
  distance. Only the four species left uncommented in 'ts_energies.txt' are involved,
  Mg2P2O7, beta-H3P2O7, alpha-H3P2O7 and H4P2O7, so the diagram is four columns wide.
  'TS_barplot_v2.py' and 'ts_levels_v2.png' were not touched.

o The canvas is now 13.333 x 7.5 inches, the 16:9 shape of a slide, saved at 300 dpi, and
  the axes box is pinned with explicit margins instead of 'tight_layout' so that the
  column geometry can be computed exactly.

o Instead of fixed font sizes the script now measures every label, every energy value and
  every column title with the real renderer and then bisects for the largest font size at
  which each column still holds its label, its bar and its value strictly inside its own
  slot. All the horizontal lengths are expressed as fractions of a column width, a
  margin of 0.03 column widths is kept clear on both sides of every dashed separator, and
  the label-bar-value block is centred inside the column so the side with the wider text
  gets the room it needs. Bars are 0.28 column widths long with the line width tied to
  the font size, and the column titles, the axis label and the tick labels are all scaled
  from the fitted size. For the present data it settles at 13.7 pt for the levels and
  18.5 pt for the column titles, against 9 pt and 13 pt before.

o Degenerate levels were the bottleneck: the 25.0 tie in beta-H3P2O7 is drawn as two
  side-by-side bars with a name on top of each, which is intrinsically wide and was
  dragging the font size of the whole figure down. Those two top labels are now allowed
  to shrink on their own, down to 70 % of the level size, so a degenerate group no longer
  caps the rest of the diagram. With this data they do not shrink at all.

o Added a verification pass that runs after the drawing: it re-measures every bar and
  every text actually placed on the axes, converts the extents back to data coordinates
  and reports the smallest clearance to a dashed separator, printing a failure line for
  anything that crosses one. The current figure clears by 0.029 column widths.

o The output is written as 'ts_levels_v3.png', so the original 'ts_levels.png' is left in
  place for comparison. A copy of the script as it stood before this pass was left as
  'TS_barplot.py.bak_20260914'.

o Also rendered 'ts_levels_v3_smallerr.png' as an alternative: it is the same script with
  the single constant ERR_SCALE set to 0.75, which prints the '+/- uncertainty' smaller
  than the energy value and, since the value block is then narrower, lets the auto-fit
  grow the levels to 16.2 pt and the column titles to 21.8 pt. Kept for a side-by-side
  look before deciding which one goes on the slide.

o The 'spread_labels' behaviour flagged in the 26/08 entry is still present in this
  script, so a side text can sit slightly off the energy of its own bar. With only these
  four species the displacement stays around 0.1 kcal/mol, which is not visible at this
  scale, but it is worth keeping in mind if more species are uncommented.

o Both renders were produced on glogin2 with the Anaconda matplotlib 3.8.0 build. A
  backup of this README as it stood before this entry was left as 'README.bak_20260914'.

15/09/2026

o The 16:9 version of the diagram was still too wide for the slide: with the name on the
  left of the bar and the energy on its right, most of each column is spent on text laid
  out horizontally and both the bars and the letters stay small. Rewrote 'TS_barplot.py'
  around a stacked layout instead. The canvas keeps its 13.333 inch width but is now 10
  inches tall, and the name and the energy of a level are written on a single line
  directly over its own bar, which frees the whole column width for the bar itself.

o The bars are now 0.80 column widths long, against 0.28 before, with a line width of
  8.2 pt against 3 pt in the original script. The auto-fit settles at 18.2 pt for the
  level text and 24.6 pt for the column titles, so with respect to the original figure
  the text is twice as large and the bars are nearly three times as long on a canvas that
  is narrower than the one we started from.

o Levels that sit too close to write both texts on the same side are not nudged any more.
  The script now enumerates, for every column, the above/below placements of the text
  blocks and keeps the first one in which no block touches another block or any bar; a
  block that cannot go above its bar is flipped below it. In practice this puts 'TS2' in
  the Mg2 column, 'TS5' in beta-H3P2O7, 'TS4' in alpha-H3P2O7 and 'TS1'/'TS2' in H4P2O7
  underneath their own bars. This also removes the 'spread_labels' displacement flagged in
  the 26/08 entry: a label now always sits at the energy of its own level.

o Degenerate levels are handled by splitting one bar into one segment per level, so the
  group keeps the footprint of a normal bar, with each name written over its own segment
  and the shared energy on the row beyond. The 25.0 tie in beta-H3P2O7 is the only case
  in this data.

o The verification pass was extended: besides checking that no bar and no text crosses a
  dashed separator, it now compares every pair of drawn objects and reports any overlap.
  The current figure passes with no overlap and a minimum clearance of 0.098 column
  widths from the separators.

o Re-ran on glogin2; 'ts_levels_v3.png' now comes out at 13.333 x 10 inches.
  'ts_levels_v3_smallerr.png' from the 14/09 pass is stale, since the ERR_SCALE constant
  no longer exists in the rewritten script, and can be deleted. 'TS_barplot_v2.py',
  'ts_levels_v2.png', 'TS_barplot.py.bak_20260914' and the original 'ts_levels.png' are
  all untouched.

15/09/2026 - 08:52

o Measured where the size of the text in this diagram actually comes from, because making
  the canvas taller had not made the figure any more readable on a slide. Sweeping the
  canvas height with everything else fixed gives 11.8 pt at 6.5 inches, 13.7 at 7.5, 18.2
  at 10 and 20.0 at 11: a straight line, 1.82 pt per inch of height. The fitted font is
  set by the vertical room each level gets, so a taller canvas buys font size and the
  scaling that fits the image back onto the slide takes exactly the same amount away.
  The 13.333 x 10 figure and the 13.333 x 7.5 one are the same size once on the slide.

o The binding constraint is the H4P2O7 column: five levels inside 5.0 kcal/mol on a
  24.7 kcal/mol axis, so the crowded window is a fifth of the axis and has to hold five
  bars and five lines of text. The width, by contrast, is slack: at 7.5 inches of height
  the fitted size stays at 13.7 pt while the canvas narrows from 13.333 to 8.5 inches.
  Reducing YPAD does not help either, 1.0 gives 13.0 pt, because the extreme levels then
  need room for their text inside the frame.

o Rebuilt the figure at a 2:5 height-to-width ratio as agreed, 13.333 x 5.333 inches. The
  auto-fit settles at 8.0 pt for the levels and 10.8 pt for the column titles, which is
  what the vertical room allows at that shape.

o Two bugs in my own fitting code came out of this, both caught by the verification pass
  rather than by eye. The block heights were taken from the height of the string 'TS1'
  and scaled, so lines carrying a superscript and a +- sign were under-estimated and
  'TS3' in H4P2O7 ended up sitting on the bar above it. Scaling a measurement from a
  20 pt reference is wrong as well: font metrics are not exactly proportional to the
  point size, and the error was about 3 percent at 9 pt, enough to put a text block on a
  bar. Every candidate size is now measured as it will actually be drawn, with a cache
  keyed on string and size since the bisection asks for the same string repeatedly.

o Recorded for later, the effective text size in a quarter-slide slot 6.67 inches wide:
  4.0 pt at the 2:5 ratio, 6.8 pt at 16:9 or 4:3, the latter being the ceiling for any
  shape in that slot. Dropping to the three lowest levels of each system gives 11.7 pt on
  the canvas, and to the two lowest 21.5 pt, at which point the text width rather than
  the vertical crowding becomes the limit. A diagram carrying all nineteen levels cannot
  be read from a quarter of a slide whatever its shape.

15/09/2026 - 09:14

o Decided to keep the diagram in the bottom left quarter of the slide with all nineteen
  levels on it, accepting that the energies are not meant to be read from the audience:
  the figure is there to show four ladders and where they sit with respect to each other,
  and the numbers are fine print for whoever looks at the slide up close.

o That decision only works if the species names and the energy scale are readable, so the
  chrome is no longer tied to the level font. Column titles, axis label and tick numbers
  now have their own fit: the titles grow to the largest size the column width allows,
  capped at 26 pt, the tick numbers and the axis label follow at 80 and 85 percent of it,
  and the left and bottom margins are then iterated until they hold them. The y-axis
  label is additionally kept shorter than the axes box, which would bite on a canvas this
  short. The levels stay at the 8.0 pt the vertical crowding allows.

o 'ts_levels_v3.png' is therefore 13.333 x 5.333 inches with 26 pt column titles, 22.1 pt
  axis label, 20.8 pt tick numbers and 8.0 pt level text. Placed in a quarter slide the
  species names land at about 13 pt and the tick numbers at 10 pt, both legible from the
  room, while the level text is around 4 pt.

o The verification pass reports no overlap and a minimum clearance of 0.100 column widths
  from the dashed separators.

17/09/2026

o Brought the whole protonation spectrum back into the diagram and reframed it as a 16:9
  figure. The six species that were commented out in both 'ts_energies.txt' and the
  SYSTEMS dictionary of 'TS_barplot.py' -- P2O7, MgP2O7, beta-HP2O7, alpha-HP2O7,
  MgHP2O7 and H2P2O7 -- are now active in both, so the plot carries all ten species and
  thirty-seven levels. Backups of both files as they stood were left as
  'ts_energies.txt.bak_20260917' and 'TS_barplot.py.bak_20260917'. The two files were
  consistent with each other beforehand, and 'ts_energies.txt' had not been touched since
  14/09; 'TS_barplot_v2.py' already had all ten species in its own dictionary, so it
  gains rather than loses from the data file being uncommented.

o Ten species did not fit at any canvas size, for two reasons that had nothing to do with
  the aspect ratio. Three P2O7 levels lie within 0.8 kcal/mol, which on this axis is
  about ten points of height, thinner than the bars themselves, so no vertical
  arrangement can separate them; and with ten columns each text line has to fit in a
  tenth of the width, which is a ratio and therefore does not improve when the canvas
  grows. That capped the level text at about 9.5 pt even with P2O7 left out.

o Both were solved by dropping what was redundant and by generalising the handling of
  degenerate levels. The species superscript of every label repeats the column title, so
  it is now stripped when drawing, 'ts_energies.txt' keeping its labels untouched; the
  uncertainty is no longer written on the figure and stays in the data file. Levels too
  close to be told apart vertically are drawn side by side at their true energies, the
  column being split into one slot per level, each slot carrying its own name with its
  own energy underneath. The script searches, for every column, every way of cutting the
  levels into consecutive clusters and keeps the cheapest one that can actually be
  placed, so a level keeps its own full width bar unless it has to share. Exactly
  degenerate levels are never split across clusters.

o Two refinements came out of measuring rather than guessing. Writing a full
  'name + energy' line inside a cluster slot does not fit, while either half of it does,
  which is why each slot gets two short rows instead: that alone took the figure from
  7.1 to 12.0 pt. And the crowded low end of H4P2O7 needs room under its lowest bar, so
  YPAD went from 2.0 to 2.5, worth another point; past 2.5 the wider axis costs more than
  the room gains.

o 'ts_levels_v3.png' is now 13.333 x 7.5 inches with 13.3 pt level text, 17.8 pt column
  titles, 15.1 pt axis label and 14.2 pt tick numbers, ten of the thirty-seven levels
  drawn side by side. The verification pass reports no overlap and a minimum clearance of
  0.100 column widths. Worth recording that ten species cost nothing in text size against
  the four-species version, which also sat at 13.7 pt: removing the two redundancies paid
  for the six extra columns.

o Correction to the backups noted above. 'TS_barplot.py.bak_20260917' did capture the
  script as it stood, that is with FIGSIZE already set to (16, 9) and all ten species
  already uncommented in the dictionary, so the only file genuinely out of step with the
  plot was 'ts_energies.txt'. 'ts_energies.txt.bak_20260917', on the other hand, came out
  holding the modified content rather than the original, so it was rebuilt by
  re-commenting the six species; it is back to 1486 bytes with its eighteen commented
  rows, matching the file as it stood on 14/09.

17/09/2026 - 10:41

o Made the species names under the columns smaller, they were heavier than they needed to
  be against the levels. The column titles now carry their own factor, TITLE_SCALE, set
  to 0.75 of the size the column width would allow, which brings them from 17.8 to
  13.3 pt, the same size as the level text. The axis label and the tick numbers are
  deliberately left out of that factor and stay at 15.1 and 14.2 pt. The bottom margin
  retightens on its own, being measured from the title height. Re-ran on glogin2, the
  verification pass still reports no overlap.
